**Definition 8.4 (publication and importer leaves).** Each `Publishj`
uses ARC Definition 7.1 **literally** with the new wrapper word. On 71
measure Base, dry-count/export candidate, append one F and seal, create
the complete descriptor. Allocate/initialize distinct full mailbox 72 and
receiver 73 payload intervals and single F delimiters. Prepare their full
68 descriptors. Immediately stage complete sender, mailbox and receiver
placement records on 69, in that order, each measured, copied, described
once and sealed. Completion records go on 71. Reacquire sender payload
length from its actual staged placement before each allocation and copy;
a staging-wire length is not a payload length.

Use native unary copies and Seek for both endpoints, then both tested
six-row payload copy bodies, final seals and TOKs, including the second
handoff. During this protected native copy/print segment no bank assignment
or extra 69 staging occurs, so the latest-three invariant remains true.
Recover actual receiver base and payload length from those records and
execute mandatory print: RD(length,5), then per 1 RD(73,4), OUT(4),
MR(73), MR(length); terminal RD(0) exits. Current receiver-read authority
comes from this actual seal/TOK, not a bit in $v_j$.

On entering each callback, **before any assignment**, use that still-latest
receiver placement to Seek(73) to its first actual bit. Then import using
RD, finite bit case, compiled literal cons, MR(73), until its existing F,
and Reverse. Helpers touch 64–68 and preserve head 73; do not use scratch
67's length across those bank rebuilds. After import staging recency is
not assumed. Later publication starts fresh measured/staged records.
ACK and mailbox return follow the current check, before any next handler.
All unchanged persistent fields are copied by every bank assignment.

